A||N||16||Justin||General Maintanance and costs?||||1||
Z||000000||Justin||08-22-2001||07:56 AM||||I am 16 and looking for my first car.  I've been looking at 86-88 GT's and I found an 86 GT with a four speed that I really like.  But I've been thinking about the cost of repairs and stuff.  I don't have tons of money to blow on this thing (since this is my first car) but what are the common things that need repaired?  And is there any expensive things I should watch out for?  Basically, I'm afraid of getting a car that has known problems, and the problems are expensive to fix.  I prefer to do the work myself (with the help of my Dad, he's a decent mechanic) but the way Fiero's are set up with the engine in the back I'm afraid we won't be able to do alot of work.  And taking a Fiero to a garage can't be cheap.  I talked to someone who owns a garage and they said that clutches in Fiero's go bad quickly and they charge more to replace them because you have to tear the engine out.  That sounds like a big hassle.  So what are some common problems and the price/difficulty in fixing them?<P>------------------<BR>If at first you don't succeed, pretend you were never trying in the first place.<P>visit <A HREF="http://www.twinkiesproject.com" TARGET=_blank>www.twinkiesproject.com</A>  for information on the chemical properties, reactions, gravitational resonse, and density issues of a Twinkie.||||reg||1
Z||000001||artherd||08-22-2001||09:17 AM||||Hiya Justin, welcome to the fourm!!<P>Well, you might get a prefectly maintained low mileage car that will need gas and oil and noting else, or you could spend twice what you paid for the car replacing nearly *everything*...   just like any car of this age.<P>How many miles does the car have? Is it in good cosmetic condition all over (inside and out, and in the front and rear bays?)<P>It is not really true that Fieros go through clutches very quickly, you can expect ~60kmiles or so out of one in general.<P>It *IS* true that they cost more to replace, don't expect it done profesionally for less than $1,000.  to do it right you do have to remove the engine.<P>The fiero is a little harder to work on than your average '67 mustang, but certinly no harder than any other car from the 1980s and later, once you get past looking for the dipstick in the front at least &lt;g&gt;<P>So, please tell us more about your car!!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Justin:<BR><B>I am 16 and looking for my first car.  I've been looking at 86-88 GT's and I found an 86 GT with a four speed that I really like.  But I've been thinking about the cost of repairs and stuff.  I don't have tons of money to blow on this thing (since this is my first car) but what are the common things that need repaired?  And is there any expensive things I should watch out for?  Basically, I'm afraid of getting a car that has known problems, and the problems are expensive to fix.  I prefer to do the work myself (with the help of my Dad, he's a decent mechanic) but the way Fiero's are set up with the engine in the back I'm afraid we won't be able to do alot of work.  And taking a Fiero to a garage can't be cheap.  I talked to someone who owns a garage and they said that clutches in Fiero's go bad quickly and they charge more to replace them because you have to tear the engine out.  That sounds like a big hassle.  So what are some common problems and the price/difficulty in fixing them?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000002||Old Lar||08-22-2001||09:18 AM||||An 86 Fiero is 15 years old and may need almost anything repaired or replaced, which will take time and money, as would any other 15 year old car.  It will all depend on how well the car was maintained in its lifetime.<P>Clutches can last 100000 or more miles.  I had my clutch replace in my 87 at 75000 miles, because I didnot want something to break when I was on the road and the car was at a shop for over a winter.  Plus I'm old and wealthy beyond belief. LOL<P>Since I farm out the repair work to people with lots of experience, they know how to drop the engine cradle to do the maintenance.  Fiero repairs are easy for those who do it routinely.<P>I would suspect in your 86 the bushings are probably shot, if they haven't been changed already. The dog bone holds the engine in place.  The EGR system and most all the sensors should be replaced as well as the plugs, wires distributor cap, pick up coil, ignition module, wheel bearings and brakes (emergency brake too, cause they don't get used that much with a manual or automatic)<P>Get a code reader to trip whatever engine codes have been stored in the computer to know at least minimally what problems may exist.  Use the forum search function to find out more about obtaining the error codes a really easy task.<BR> <BR>The front exhaust manifold may be cracked and leaking, but if you drop the engine, that could be an easy fix with a MIG welder.<P>If you purchase the car get the Haynes Manual to lead you through the trouble shooting and repair directions.<P>The engines themselve,if maintained, can last 150000-250000 miles, however I bought a used Fiero with 54000 and a beat engine, which required replacement.<P>There are lots of people in Pa with Fiero experience.  Go to the Fiero web ring or the vendor list on the forum and look for the vendors and parts suppliers.  The only one that come to my mind right now is Waynes World in Allentown.<P>There were a bunch of Fieros this past weekend at Zinn's Diner in Denver at the Fiero Dutch Classic II meet.  You missed a chance to see many well maintained Fieros.<p>[This message has been edited by Old Lar (edited 08-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Justin||08-22-2001||10:45 AM||||The Fiero I have picked out is an 86 GT with 127,000 miles.  It is at a dealer, and it just so happens the dealer is a Auto Detailing place too (I think they are called Top Dog Auto Detailing and Sales).  So I know it will be clean.  It is red with the silver stripe on the bottom.  I haven't seen it in person yet, but the paint looks good from the picture.  It has a sunroof and power everything.  The clutch was replaced by them, which means it probably has under 20 miles on it.  They want $1995 for it.  I'm gonna go check it out soon.  Does that sound like a good deal?<P>------------------<BR>If at first you don't succeed, pretend you were never trying in the first place.<P>visit <A HREF="http://www.twinkiesproject.com" TARGET=_blank>www.twinkiesproject.com</A>  for information on the chemical properties, reactions, gravitational resonse, and density issues of a Twinkie.||||reg||1
Z||000004||GodSend||08-22-2001||11:42 AM||||Dont let these guys scare you.  Seems everyone is trying to prepair you for the worst.<P>  Just make sure you get a mechanic to give  a nice inspection.  I bought my 87 gt over a year ago and I have only done select things to it (here is a break down with prices in Canadian $)<P>New front break pads - 50$ installed<BR>New o2 Sensor - 35 (installed my self, easy)<BR>New Battery - 100$ <BR>New Alt Belt - 17$ (installed myself, easy)<P>and thats it.. she runs great.<P>||||reg||14
Z||000005||fierobaby||08-22-2001||11:49 AM||||Justin,<P>I am 17 and have my first car. 1984 Fiero 87 thousand miles on it. I have spent almost 1,000 into fixing up my car...unless you have money to spare be very carefull with what you get. I am not saying don't buy the Fiero just be sure its in good shape and Check the brakelines very carfully!!! I know that from experiance brake jobs are EXPENSIVE!<P>Corinne<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="LETTER"> AIM: Fierobaby87<BR>http://angelfire.com/sports/sfiero </FONT><BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cfiero.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||EdsB52||08-22-2001||11:51 AM||||I would suggest a Fiero is not a good first car. Even if you have a support team to help you/repair your car for you. Virtually everything is a pain to work on, and if you farm out the maintenance, plan on digging deep. If you decide to run the piss out of it, plan on often maintenance. I love Fieros and am not trying to dissuade you from buying a neat car, just trying to help you avoid a nightmare.||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||08-22-2001||02:09 PM||||the worse things and most expensive, most common are clutch working right, not overheating, exhaust leaks. If those are Ok and it runs ok after an extended test drive go for it. Maybe even ask if you leave a deposit to drive it for a weekend. Lots of dealers will do that if you dont look like you just want to joyride it. Maybe you could get your dad to do that for you, they may not like giving it to a younger person.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Voytek||08-22-2001||03:30 PM||||Hey!<P>My second car was a Fiero - I was 18 and the car was 8 years old (in '93).  I've had my share of problems but I learned on the car.  Now I have an '87 GT and still learning some things.<P>To answer your question, though, a Fiero is NOT a hard car to work on UNLESS you need to do anything to the tranny or clutch (or worse, internal engine parts).  Everything else is no worse than any other car, and much easier than many cars.  I am no mechanic and feel that I can fix my Fiero better than most shops (unless the enginer needs to come out).<P>Let me give you an example.  A water pump for the Fiero is $27 CDN (rebuilt) and takes about 1.5 hours to replace yourself.<P>An A/C compressor took me about 45 minutes to take out (I don't have a cat converter) and about 1.5 hours to install.<P>If the clutch is done on this car (and do ask for a warranty!!!!!!!!!), it doesn't seem to overheat (check this one for sure!!!), and of'course the engine doesn't sound funny - buy it!<P>These cars share many parts with other GM cars from the 80's making the replacement costs relatively low.<P>Good luck and let us know if you bought it!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Jncomutt||08-22-2001||03:55 PM||||Well I'm 17 too... My Fiero was my 1st car.  Picked it up for $250 and got about 20k miles out of it keeping my parts budget under 1000.  My 84 is also my 2nd Fiero.  Picked it up for $400.  Only 56k miles.  It needed all brakes (did myself, not too hard), Then General stuff like plugs, wires, rotor, cap, coil, ignition, fuel filter, air filter...  All cheap, and running great.  I'm running into the slipping clutch problem which I'm thinking is going to set me back some $$$, but all otherwise, I would say theres no other car I'd be happier with for 1st and 2nd.  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||GodSend||08-22-2001||06:58 PM||||If you think a fiero is hard to work on, I don't want to hear your language after working on a Talon TSI or a 90+ 300zx  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||Justin||08-22-2001||09:18 PM||||Hmmm... Mixed reactions.  Not exactly what I was hoping for.  Well, I'm just gonna go look at it, and if it looks good, probably get it.  Thanks for the help everybody.<P>BTW: Skitime, if your reading this, just wanted to say your Fiero looks real nice.  The picture just doesn't show how great of a car it really is.  I'd say the wheels really finish the car.  I printed out a picture of your car and when I tell my friends that I might be getting a Fiero and they laugh I just show them the picture, that shuts them up real quick!<P>------------------<BR>If at first you don't succeed, pretend you were never trying in the first place.<P>visit <A HREF="http://www.twinkiesproject.com" TARGET=_blank>www.twinkiesproject.com</A>  for information on the chemical properties, reactions, gravitational resonse, and density issues of a Twinkie.||||reg||1
